  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1146 AM MDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nELEVATED FLASH FLOODING THREAT THROUGH MONDAY:\n\nToday's weather pattern is very similar to yesterday's with an \nupper- level jet draped along an axis from southwest Utah to \ncentral Wyoming. Locally, the jet will provide synoptic support \nfor continued showers and storms along and north of I-70. The \nnear- surface layer is more saturated today, so outflow winds \nwill not be as impactful today. Stronger storms will be capable \nof producing 35- 45 mph gusts and frequent lightning. \n\nWith widespread precipitation expected in the same locations as\nyesterday, the threat for flash flooding will be higher today, \nespecially in locations that receive multiple rounds of showers\nand storms. The same can be said for tomorrow, with flooding \nbecoming more and more likely as soils and streams take on \nconsecutive days of rainfall. Today, the potential for flash \nflooding will be along and north of I-70. Tomorrow, with richer \nmoisture moving into the Four Corners region, the threat will \nprimarily be along and south of I-70. Recent burn scars are \nespecially susceptible to heavy rainfall which may cause debris \nflows and flash flooding regardless of location. Due to these \nreasons, a Flash Flood Watch is in effect for much of eastern \nUtah and western Colorado through Monday evening. Please see the\nHydrology section below for more information. \n\n\nLOOKING AHEAD: \n\nA drier airmass moves in on Tuesday and remains in place for \nmuch of the work week. Afternoon showers and storms remain in \nthe forecast over the higher terrain, but coverage will be much \nmore isolated. Our first week of seasonable temperatures in \nquite some time will be the highlight of the week.\n\nEnsemble guidance supports another push of monsoonal mositure \nacross the Intermountain West next weekend. More details to come\nas the week progresses, but keep an eye on the forecast if you \nhave outdoor plans next weekend.",
